WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.190
Part 5 of nine: Connect your role with organizational responsibility.

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.360
The standards connected with this part are 2, 3, 4,

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:08.580
9.

4
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.290
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Identify

5
00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.410
what you can change, who must approve and what support

6
00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:15.990
is needed

7
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.080
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 2 is described as:

8
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.000
Sustained leadership, policy, practice and resources.

9
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.250
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 3 is described as:

10
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.450
A workforce equipped and supported to respond to population needs.

11
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.100
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 4 is described as:

12
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.140
Regular workforce and leadership learning.

13
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.550
Individual action needs support.

14
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.500
We've found possible improvements. How do we connect them with

15
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.140
the people and resources needed to make them dependable?

16
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.930
So far, you have identified a work example, asked about

17
00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:57.130
barriers, traced assistance and considered the information itself. Some improvements

18
00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.270
may be within your control. Others depend on authority, resources

19
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.050
or coordination. Recognizing that distinction is part of effective practice.

20
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.280
Standards two, three and four address governance, leadership and workforce.

21
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.260
Standard nine connects goals, policies and accountability to operations. Together,

22
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.600
they remind us that culturally and linguistically appropriate services cannot

23
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.040
depend only on one person's effort or goodwill.

24
00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.470
In this lesson, choose the perspective closest to your responsibilities.

25
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.870
You can also explore the others. The examples describe possible

26
00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.470
contributions, not a new assignment of duties. Use your actual

27
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.490
role, current procedures and supervisory guidance to determine what you

28
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:35.870
can decide.

29
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.980
If your contribution is an everyday task.

30
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.850
Let's start with someone who owns an everyday task but

31
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.150
doesn't approve the whole process.

32
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.940
You might prepare information, answer questions, support a meeting, review

33
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.600
a document, analyze a process or identify a recurring problem.

34
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.460
Your contribution can begin with careful observation, a respectful question,

35
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.620
a well-supported suggestion and follow-through on the work you own.

36
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.980
For the sample notice, you may be able to identify

37
00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.460
unclear instructions and propose a revision. You may not be

38
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.280
authorized to change a required deadline or replace an approved

39
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.620
notice. A useful next step is to describe the barrier,

40
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.140
preserve the required meaning and send the proposed improvement to

41
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:17.860
the person responsible.

42
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.820
Avoid carrying an unresolved access problem privately from one encounter

43
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.960
to the next. Use the appropriate route to identify repeated

44
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.000
difficulties, while protecting personal information. Raising a pattern with clear

45
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.200
evidence gives others something they can act on.

46
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.690
If you supervise people.

47
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.360
How could a supervisor help staff use this learning in

48
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:38.940
real decisions?

49
00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.870
A supervisor can help staff prepare for access needs, locate

50
00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.310
procedures and practice difficult conversations. Coaching can begin with questions:

51
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.600
what did you observe, what did you ask, what remains

52
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.530
uncertain, and what support do you need?

53
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.940
Discuss the work without reducing the person receiving services to

54
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.040
a label. Help staff distinguish an access barrier from an

55
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.920
assumption about motivation. Make room for questions and appropriate escalation.

56
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.780
A person who identifies a gap should not have to

57
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.540
pretend they already know how to resolve it.

58
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.030
Standard four addresses regular education and training. A useful learning

59
00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.690
opportunity connects to the work staff do and the choices

60
00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.470
they face. A brief review of a notice or an

61
00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.230
assistance request can be more useful than repeating a definition

62
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.410
without practicing its use.

63
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.080
If you manage a process or resources.

64
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.510
And what changes when someone manages a process or resources?

65
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.720
A manager may be able to examine recurring patterns, clarify

66
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.880
ownership, allocate available resources and coordinate changes. For our notice,

67
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.320
that could mean identifying who maintains the text, who arranges

68
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.700
assistance, how requests are tracked and how problems are reviewed.

69
00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.250
Ask whether the proposed process is dependable when the usual

70
00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.510
contact is unavailable. Consider workload and capacity. An instruction to

71
00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.590
offer assistance is incomplete if staff have no workable way

72
00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:03.470
to arrange it. An improvement plan needs an owner, appropriate

73
00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.290
resources and a way to evaluate whether it works.

74
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.400
Do not interpret a difference in participation as proof of

75
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.020
one cause. Use it as a reason to investigate. The

76
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.780
evidence lesson will show how to combine operational information with

77
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:18.900
experience and context.

78
00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:22.310
If you set direction or advise leadership.

79
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.380
What should leaders, managers and directors consider when they set

80
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.900
direction or prepare a recommendation?

81
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:34.750
Leadership responsibilities include making expectations visible in policy, planning, resources

82
00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:40.330
and accountability. Standard two addresses sustained organizational support. Standard three

83
00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.330
addresses a workforce and leadership equipped to respond to the

84
00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.090
needs of the population.

85
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.060
This should not become an assumption that one employee represents

86
00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.840
an entire community or should provide informal interpreting because of

87
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.620
their identity. Capability, preparation, appropriate role assignments and support matter.

88
00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.500
Recruitment, development and promotion practices should follow applicable requirements while

89
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.540
considering the skills and experience needed to serve people effectively.

90
00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.610
Someone advising leadership can frame a decision clearly: here is

91
00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.570
the access problem, here is the evidence and uncertainty, here

92
00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:15.210
are the options, and here is what each option requires.

93
00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.610
The recommendation should explain who will be affected and how

94
00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.250
their perspectives will inform the decision.

95
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:26.560
The current HHS standard three connects recruiting, advancing, preparing and

96
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.180
supporting governance, leaders and staff with the digital, cultural and

97
00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.720
language needs of the population. It addresses the capabilities and

98
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.680
conditions around the work.

99
00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:36.890
Map the next step.

100
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.360
Can we turn those different responsibilities into a practical next-step

101
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:41.660
map?

102
00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:47.030
For your work example, separate three categories. First, what can

103
00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:51.830
you change within your responsibilities? Second, what requires coordination or

104
00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.490
review? Third, what needs a decision by someone with different

105
00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:55.750
authority?

106
00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.380
For each proposed action, identify the responsible role rather than

107
00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.620
inventing a person's name. Add the resource or information needed

108
00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.220
and a reasonable next step. If ownership is unclear, clarifying

109
00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.440
ownership can itself be the next action.

110
00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.650
In the practice activity, choose the response that advances the

111
00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:18.070
improvement without exceeding authority or abandoning the concern. Neither changing

112
00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.250
an official requirement on your own nor leaving the problem

113
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.030
unraised is a complete response.

114
00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.300
Connect responsibility with participation.

115
00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.490
Who else needs to influence the plan before the decision

116
00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:29.930
is settled?

117
00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:34.080
Your role map makes the improvement more realistic. It also

118
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.860
reveals who has not yet been heard. In the next

119
00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.420
lesson, we will consider how the people affected can inform

120
00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.620
a change before the decision is already made.

121
00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:46.510
Let's work through the companion example: Turn a concern into

122
00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.670
a supported proposal

123
00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:52.220
Here is the situation. You can identify unclear wording but

124
00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.240
cannot approve a change to the official notice.

125
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.490
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

126
00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.320
Prepare a concise description of the barrier, a suggested revision

127
00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:06.780
that preserves the requirement, the relevant access questions and a

128
00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.840
request for review by the responsible role.

129
00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:13.030
And here is why that response fits. A specific proposal

130
00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:16.250
moves the work forward without changing requirements outside your authority.

131
00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.360
Now make your own choice. You identify a barrier in

132
00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.140
an official notice but do not have authority to change

133
00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.520
the requirement. What is a useful response?

134
00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.850
Option 1. Change the requirement yourself because the goal is

135
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:30.230
important.

136
00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.660
Option 2. Leave the issue unraised because you cannot approve

137
00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:35.100
the change.

138
00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.190
Option 3. Describe the barrier, propose a revision that preserves

139
00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:42.750
the required meaning and identify the right person.

140
00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.100
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

141
00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.120
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

142
00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.400
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

143
00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.500
through all three responses.

144
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.270
How should we think about option 1?

145
00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.300
An important goal does not expand your authority. Preserve the

146
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.500
requirement and route a supported proposal to the person responsible.

147
00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.230
How should we think about option 2?

148
00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:10.300
You can still document the barrier and recommend a next

149
00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.880
step. Raising a well-described problem is a meaningful contribution.

150
00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.530
How should we think about option 3?

151
00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.180
This advances the work responsibly. Clarify ownership, resources and the

152
00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.360
next review step rather than assuming approval.

153
00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.870
Option 3 is the supported choice in this activity.

154
00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.980
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

155
00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.570
What can you change directly? What needs coordination? What requires

156
00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:40.670
another person's decision? Identify roles, resources and a next step.

157
00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.680
The working note from this part is: Responsibility map

158
00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.050
Pause the player if you want time to make that

159
00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:51.850
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

160
00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.930
it through without writing is also fine.

161
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.440
That completes part 5. You can return to any passage

162
00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.500
or take a break. The next part is Learn with

163
